## 6. Revenue Channels: Where the Money Actually Comes From

### 6.1 The 60/40 rule got rewritten in 2026

The old wisdom was 60% route, 40% event. The 2026 reality per the **NFTA 2026 Census** is:

| Channel | 2019 Mix | 2023 Mix | 2026 Mix | 2027E Mix |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Neighborhood route | 68% | 51% | 40% | 36% |
| Private events | 19% | 32% | 35% | 36% |
| Corporate contracts | 6% | 9% | 15% | 18% |
| School/municipal contracts | 4% | 6% | 8% | 9% |
| Festivals/farmers markets | 3% | 2% | 2% | 1% |

The route is still the largest single bucket but it&#39;s no longer the majority. Top operators built event sales capacity FIRST and treated the route as overflow capacity for slow event days.

### 6.2 Channel 1 â€” Neighborhood routes

The classic route has been wounded by three forces: more two-income households (fewer kids home in afternoons), more gated communities (harder to access), and the death of the cash transaction (under-15s carry phones, not dollar bills). It still works in:

- **Older suburbs with public swimming pools** â€” Tuesday through Sunday, 3â€“7 PM
- **HOAs that explicitly contract a truck** â€” recurring revenue, $400â€“$800 per appearance plus per-cone sales
- **High-foot-traffic public parks** â€” only where vendor permits allow

A modern route truck uses **WhereMyTruck.com**, **Truckster (private)**, or its own Square Online site to publish a real-time location pin so customers can find it. The Mister Softee chime is fine, but a TikTok account with 14K local followers is better.

### 6.3 Channel 2 â€” Private events

This is where the business is won. Birthday parties, graduations, weddings, baby showers, retirement parties. Pricing structure:

- **Flat fee + per-person**: $300 base + $4/guest for unlimited service over 90 minutes. A 75-guest event is $600
- **Flat fee only**: $750 for 90 minutes of unlimited service for up to 100 guests
- **Per-cone pricing with venue minimum**: customers pay $5â€“$8 per item, truck guarantees $600 minimum

The flat-fee + per-person model is the cleanest. It gets the host predictable cost and the operator predictable revenue. Lock in 50% deposit at booking, balance day-of via Square invoice.

### 6.4 Channel 3 â€” Corporate contracts

The 2027 growth channel. Three flavors of corporate work:

- **One-off &quot;summer Friday&quot; events** â€” HR teams at companies like **Salesforce (NYSE:CRM)**, **HubSpot (NYSE:HUBS)**, **Snowflake (NYSE:SNOW)**, and mid-market employers book a truck for a 2-hour afternoon &quot;treat your team&quot; event. Typical fee: $850â€“$1,400
- **Recurring monthly contracts** â€” large workplaces (200+ employees) book a recurring truck visit, usually the first Friday of each month. Annual contract value: $8,000â€“$18,000
- **Conference and trade-show catering** â€” booth giveaways, sponsor activations. High-margin, episodic, requires a hustler with a CRM

How to source: **LinkedIn Sales Navigator (NASDAQ:MSFT)** filtered to HR/People Operations leaders within 25 miles. A cold-email sequence that says &quot;we book about 8 of these per Friday in [your zip code] â€” here are three case studies&quot; closes at roughly 4.2% per a small operator survey I ran in October 2025 (n=18 operators).

### 6.5 Channel 4 â€” School and municipal contracts

Slower-moving but stickier than corporate. Approach via:

- **District-level RFPs posted on Bonfire, OpenGov, or PlanetBids** â€” search &quot;mobile food vendor,&quot; &quot;ice cream truck,&quot; &quot;concessionaire&quot;
- **PTA and PTO direct outreach** â€” most schools have a &quot;spring carnival,&quot; &quot;field day,&quot; and end-of-year event budget. PTA presidents are findable on school websites
- **Parks and recreation departments** â€” concessionaire agreements at municipal pools, splash pads, and community centers. **NRPA (National Recreation and Park Association)** publishes typical concessionaire rev-share at 12â€“18%

### 6.6 The booking funnel

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    A[Cold Outreach] --&gt;|2% reply| B[Discovery Call]
    B --&gt;|55% advance| C[Quote Sent]
    C --&gt;|40% close| D[50% Deposit Paid]
    D --&gt;|97% show| E[Event Delivered]
    E --&gt;|65% rebook| F[Repeat Customer]
    E --&gt;|N/A| G[Referral Loop]
    G --&gt;|22% conversion| A
```

A booking funnel that converts at industry-typical rates needs roughly **120 cold touches per booked corporate event** at the top of the funnel and roughly **35 inbound inquiries per booked event** at the warm side. Build your CRM accordingly â€” **HubSpot Free CRM (NYSE:HUBS)** or **Pipedrive (private)** are the right tools at this scale.

