## 13. Geographic Market Analysis: Where to Launch in 2027

### 13.1 The seasonality map

Ice cream truck economics are violently sensitive to season length. A 46-week season generates roughly 1.9x the revenue of a 24-week season at the same daily run-rate, but it does NOT generate 1.9x the operating income, because fixed costs (insurance, commissary, loan service) accrue all 52 weeks regardless. The viable-season map for the US in 2026:

| Market Tier | Example Cities | Viable Season | Year-1 Realistic Revenue (Base Case) | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Tier A (44+ weeks) | Phoenix, Tampa, Miami, Las Vegas, Houston, San Diego, Orlando | 44â€“48 weeks | $245Kâ€“$320K | Best gross revenue; competitive intensity highest |
| Tier B (34â€“42 weeks) | Atlanta, Dallas, Charlotte, Nashville, Sacramento, Austin, LA | 34â€“42 weeks | $185Kâ€“$245K | Sweet spot â€” long enough season, lighter competition than A |
| Tier C (28â€“33 weeks) | DC, Philadelphia, St. Louis, Kansas City, Denver, Indianapolis | 28â€“33 weeks | $135Kâ€“$185K | Solid markets; require strong event book |
| Tier D (22â€“27 weeks) | Chicago, Boston, NYC, Detroit, Pittsburgh, Cincinnati | 22â€“27 weeks | $98Kâ€“$135K | Viable but tight; almost all revenue must be event-led |
| Tier E (&lt;22 weeks) | Minneapolis, Milwaukee, Buffalo, Seattle, Portland OR, Anchorage | 16â€“21 weeks | $58Kâ€“$98K | Side-hustle viable; tough to be primary income |

### 13.2 Local market diligence before you commit

Before committing capital to a market, do these five checks:

- **Permit availability** â€” call the city health department and ask: how many active mobile food vendor permits exist, what is the issuance cap (if any), and what is the lead time. Some cities (San Francisco, Boston) cap permits; some (Austin, Nashville) do not
- **Competitor density** â€” search **Google Maps** within a 15-mile radius for &quot;ice cream truck,&quot; &quot;soft serve catering,&quot; &quot;mobile dessert.&quot; Count operators. Above 8 operators in a metro under 600K population is saturated
- **Commissary availability** â€” call **CommissaryConnect.com** and your local restaurant association. If there are fewer than 3 commissaries within 25 miles, you have a structural cost problem
- **Event venue density** â€” count corporate office parks, schools, HOAs, and event venues. A spreadsheet of 200+ named target venues within 25 miles is the minimum for a viable launch
- **Local TAM math** â€” population density Ã— disposable income Ã— season length Ã— $14 per-capita annual mobile dessert spend (NFTA benchmark) = local TAM. Take 0.3% local share as a Year-1 target

### 13.3 Underrated 2027 launch markets

Three markets that look underbuilt to capacity per **U.S. Census Bureau SUSB data 2024** crossed with **NFTA operator counts**:

- **Greenville, SC** â€” population 1.2M metro, only 6 active operators, Tier B season length, strong corporate base (BMW, Michelin)
- **Boise, ID** â€” population 800K metro, 4 active operators, Tier B/C season, rapid population growth (+3.1% CAGR)
- **Chattanooga, TN** â€” population 575K metro, 3 active operators, Tier B season, growing tech/logistics employer base

